FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs:145 — the tree this delivery commits fails the project's own audit test the moment the pipeline creates the delivery commit. The `ACKNOWLEDGED` entry names `18c0c46176c51e8a4168f87adfb252ada12ebaed`, which is the commit that carried the *previous* round's work; `phases/phase-084/FINDINGS.md:1316-1319` states the mechanism plainly — the pipeline "re-creates the delivery commit on each round … rebuilt on the same parent `b21821d`", so the previous round's SHA leaves `HEAD`'s ancestry. After the commit, `is_unreviewed_subject` (tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs:445) selects the new commit (its subject is `resume phase-084: adopt preserved work`), `scan` reports it as unreviewed (:488-503) and reports `18c0c46` as stale (:504-509), and `unreviewed_work_in_head_history_is_acknowledged` asserts both lists empty (:558, :567) — two failures. This matters because the report's headline gate number is a measurement of the *pre-commit* tree: `phases/phase-084/REPORT.md:88` ("1952 passed; 0 failed") and the pasted gate output at REPORT.md:807-808 ("PASS cargo test: 1952 passed, 0 failed") do not describe the tree that gets committed. It is disclosed (REPORT.md:889-898) and the phase's own fix — the dispatcher writes the `ACKNOWLEDGED` line at commit time, where the SHA is known — has not been made, so this is the twelfth round to pay it. Concrete fix: in the pipeline's commit step, append the just-created commit's SHA to `ACKNOWLEDGED` (or stop the resume-commit subject from matching the selector); alternatively make the audit's delivery-commit rule explicit so a rebuilt delivery commit is neither unreviewed nor stale by construction. I could not run the pipeline's gate (no `bash` in this contract), so what would settle it is one run of `rbops/verify.sh phase-084` against the committed tree: if the gate runs after the commit it fails on this commit, and if it runs before, the committed tree is red either way.
2. [MINOR] phases/phase-084/REPORT.md:135 — the report's own diff tables do not match the diff being shipped. REPORT.md:135 says `phases/phase-084/REPORT.md` is `+245 −60` for this round, and REPORT.md:124 says `tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs` is `+62 −2` for "the delivery as a whole, measured against the base … (b21821d)"; the numstat of this change set is `247 62` for REPORT.md and `63 3` for the acknowledgement file (committed `62 2` plus this round's `1 1`). REPORT.md:764 asserts "Every number in this table was measured in this round", and the pasted gate line "diff substance: +4870 -2" (REPORT.md:798) equals the sum of the report's own (stale) table, not of the tree. Concrete fix: re-derive both rows from `git diff --numstat b21821d` and `git diff --numstat 18c0c46` at the moment the report is final, and note that the REPORT.md row is self-referential (stating it changes it) — the acknowledgement-file row is not, and is simply missing this round's `+1 −1`.
3. [MINOR] tests/agent_permissions_test.rs:1839 — `project_config_accounts_for_every_permission_key_opencode_knows` cannot detect the thing its doc and the report say it detects. It asserts `REVIEWER_DENIED ∪ REVIEWER_UNDECLARED == PERMISSION_KEYS`, and all three are hand-copied constants in the same file (:118, :151, :167), so a key opencode adds tomorrow leaves the test green — contradicting its own doc (:161-166: "a key that opencode adds tomorrow is in neither of them — which is where that shows up") and REPORT.md:73-74 ("a key opencode adds tomorrow is a key that shows up in exactly one place"). The phase's own live measurement already shows the enumeration is not opencode's full set: the resolved ruleset pasted at REPORT.md:416-417 contains `plan_enter * deny` and `plan_exit * deny`, two permission entries that are in none of the three lists. The reviewer's block is still closed against them (`check_no_grants_beyond` refuses any key outside `REVIEWER_DENIED`, :521), so the security effect is nil; the defect is that the round presents this test as one of its two substantive closures and the test's name and doc make a guarantee it does not deliver. Concrete fix: rename the test to what it asserts (the two lists partition the constant), move the "a key opencode adds tomorrow" claim out of the doc, and either drop `plan_enter`/`plan_exit` from the claim or state that the fifteen are the keys the schema types as configurable while opencode resolves two further internal entries.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 3
